lesson: 78
title: Two steps: some come, some go
stars: 1
explain: Some stories have two changes: first some things come, then some go away, or the other way round.
explain: Do one step at a time, in the order of the story: 12 stickers, get 5 more, give away 7: 12 + 5 = 17, then 17 - 7 = 10.
explain: Adding and then taking away gives the same as taking away and then adding, so 12 - 7 + 5 = 10 too.
vars: a=name b=name c=name t=thing n=5..60 m=2..40 k=2..40
need: n > k
need: n + m - k >= 2
answer: n + m - k
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {b} gave {a.him} {m} more. Then {a} gave {k} {t} to {c}. How many {t} does {a} have now?
exercise: There were {n} {t} in a box. {a} put in {m} more and then took out {k}. How many {t} are in the box now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {a.He} gave {k} to {b} and then bought {m} more. How many {t} does {a} have now?
exercise: A shop had {n} {t}. In the morning it sold {k}. In the afternoon {m} more {t} arrived. How many {t} does the shop have now?
exercise: {n} children were on a bus. At the first stop {m} children got on and {k} got off. How many children are on the bus now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {a.He} lost {k} of them, but then {b} gave {a.him} {m}. How many {t} does {a} have now?
test: {a} gets {m} {t} from {b} and passes {k} on to {c}. If {a} began with {n}, how many {t} does {a.he} end with?
test: Before lunch a jar held {n} {t}. {k} were taken out at lunch, and {m} were put back in after it. How many {t} are in the jar?
test: {k} {t} left {a}'s bag and {m} new ones went in. The bag had {n} at the start. What does it hold now?
test: A library shelf had {n} books. Children borrowed {k} and returned {m}. How many books are on the shelf now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {a.He} got {m} more from {b} and handed out {k} to {c}. How many {t} does {a} have left?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have now if {a.he} started with {n}, received {m} and gave away {k}?
exercise: A bus in Chicago left with {n} people. At the next stop {k} people got off and {m} got on. How many people are on the bus now?
exercise: A farm in Iowa had {n} hens. The farmer sold {k} hens and bought {m} new ones. How many hens are on the farm now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {a.He} donated {k} to a school, and then {b} gave {a.him} {m}. How many {t} does {a} still have?
exercise: There were {n} {t} in a box in a classroom in Leeds. The teacher took out {k} and later put in {m}. How many {t} are in the box at the end?
exercise: {a} began the day with {n} {t}, won {m} more in a game and lost {k} on the way home. How many {t} remain?
exercise: A car park in Brisbane had {n} cars. Then {m} cars came in and {k} cars drove out. How many cars are in the car park now?
test: There are {n} {t}; then {m} more come and {k} go. How many {t} are there now?
test: How many ducks are on a pond in Ottawa now, if there were {n}, {k} flew away and {m} landed?
test: {b} handed {a} {m} {t}; before that {a} had {n}. Afterwards {a} passed {k} to {c}. How many {t} does {a} hold now?
test: A hotel in Cardiff had {n} guests. {k} guests checked out and {m} new guests checked in. How many guests are at the hotel now?
test: {a}'s piggy bank held {n} dollars. {a.He} spent {k} dollars and then put in {m} dollars more. How many dollars are in the piggy bank?
test: On a tram in Melbourne there were {n} passengers; {m} got on and then {k} got off. How many passengers were left on the tram?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}, got {m} off {b}, gave {k} to {c}. how many {a} got now
exercise: {n} kids on the bus, {m} got on, {k+1} got off — no wait, {k} got off. how many on now
exercise: box had {n} {t}, {a} chucked {m} more in n took {k} out. hows many in there now
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}, lost {k}, then {b} gave {a.him} {m}. my brother says thats {n+m+k}. nah whats the real number
test: shop had {n} {t}, sold {k} in the morning, {m} more come in the arvo. how many they got now
test: {a} started with {n} {t}, won {m}, lost {k} walkin home, oh and it rained like 2 hours. how many left
test: {n} ducks on the pond, {k} flew off n {m} landed. {a} reckons {n-k} then?? not right innit, how many ducks now
exam: Whereas {a} originally held {n} {t}, subsequently received {m} from {b}, and thereafter surrendered {k} to {c}, determine {a}'s final holding.
exam: It is given that a receptacle containing {n} {t} is first augmented by {m} {t} and subsequently diminished by {k} {t}. Ascertain its final contents.
exam: Compute the result of adding {m.words} to {n.words} and subtracting {k.words} from the sum so obtained.
exam: State the number of {t} remaining with {a} pursuant to the receipt of {m} {t} and the subsequent disposal of {k} {t}, {a.his} initial stock having been {n}.
exercise: The farm had {n} chickens. {m} chicks hatched, then the fox got in and {k} chickens went to the big henhouse in the sky. How many chickens are there now?
exercise: Mum had {n} dollars. Grandma, who passed away in May, left her {m} dollars, and Mum spent {k} on flowers for the funeral. How many dollars does Mum have now?
test: dad's 'between jobs' so: had {n} quid, got {m} from the dole, then the bloody gas bill took {k}. how much left
